swer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-129176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-129176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-47923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[0.5,1]$\n- $[-1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-47923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-47923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5b4b5edbd5b2394065fe8e21429035bbfd6c134115ba9f7d7be4669829acb248"} {"question" "q-47923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-47923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-47923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n- $[-1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73495" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5b4b5edbd5b2394065fe8e21429035bbfd6c134115ba9f7d7be4669829acb248"} {"question" "q-73495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73495" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $(0,3]$\n- $[-2,-1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5b4b5edbd5b2394065fe8e21429035bbfd6c134115ba9f7d7be4669829acb248"} {"question" "q-73503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-131066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n- $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n- $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-131066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-131066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5b4b5edbd5b2394065fe8e21429035bbfd6c134115ba9f7d7be4669829acb248"} {"question" "q-131066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-131066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-131066" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[2,\\pi ]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5b4b5edbd5b2394065fe8e21429035bbfd6c134115ba9f7d7be4669829acb248"} {"question" "q-73504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II  only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "q-73504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c20f2a6145366ef0a982e1edfd2b34c5fe66d2f7838c0e02db356cd0f04f919"} {"question" "e-3733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/66cd2a8b7e88849efc1b8275fd35f763984e4444821a9cd0e0e9060a8f90c956.png)\n\n    \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-2,1]$\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $[-4,4]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/example-3733.json" "file_sha256" "3e342e2dfc2deeaae54e84c9f2f8e1e5a7e78498b42e40e0134b24c9a94e6ab1"} {"question" "e-3733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-2,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-2,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-4,4]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-4,4]$ because there are discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ both of which lie within $[-4,4].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,1]$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/example-3733.json" "file_sha256" "3e342e2dfc2deeaae54e84c9f2f8e1e5a7e78498b42e40e0134b24c9a94e6ab1"} {"question" "e-3735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x}+2\\right)?$ \n\n- $[0,2]$\n\n- $(0,2)$\n\n- $[-2,0]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/example-3735.json" "file_sha256" "0726197571bb11f06db97592376bacd83dc50cd0889533aa410b8758f3296a71"} {"question" "e-3735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-2,0]$ because this interval contains points where $\\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x}+2\\right)$ is not defined. In particular, $\\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative values of $x.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0,2]$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/example-3735.json" "file_sha256" "0726197571bb11f06db97592376bacd83dc50cd0889533aa410b8758f3296a71"} {"question" "e-3732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIs the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$ on the intervals $[-1,2]$ and $[-1,0]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/example-3732.json" "file_sha256" "493fa88b2068c6b6e3906e6159d7e62d430fb622b2c1356b6f397135e1f3ce1d"} {"question" "e-3732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-1}=+\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is *not* continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[-1,0].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval $[-1,0].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-1,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, of the two intervals, the extreme value theorem is only applicable on $[-1,0].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/example-3732.json" "file_sha256" "493fa88b2068c6b6e3906e6159d7e62d430fb622b2c1356b6f397135e1f3ce1d"}] "usage" {"e-3733" [] "e-3732" [] "e-3735" [] "q-47780" []} "basis" 897 "retractions" [[17592186176412 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3571/Source/Images/q-47780.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$?\n\n1. $[- 1, 2]$\n2. $[MATH: (1, 3]]$\n3. $[- 4,-1]$"] [17592186175690 :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3571/Source/Images/3b8ef5690fbe8b89c561afb3ee490431.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$?\n\n1. $[-2,1]$\n2. $(-2,1)$\n3. $[-4,4]$"] [17592186175690 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-2,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,1]$. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n- The interval $(-2,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n- The interval $[-4,4]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-4,4]$ because there are discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=2$, both of which lie within $[-4,4]$. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,1]$ only."] [17592186175691 :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x}+2\\right)$?\n\n1. $[0,2]$\n2. $(0,2)$\n3. $[-2,0]$"] [17592186175691 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,2]$. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n- The interval $(0,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n- The interval $[-2,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-2,0]$ because this interval contains points where $\\ln\\left(\\sqrt{x}+2\\right)$ is not defined. In particular, $\\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative values of $x$. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0,2]$ only."] [17592186175689 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n${\\frac{1}{x - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$.\nIs the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$ on the intervals $[-1,2]$ and $[-1,0]$?"] [17592186175689 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,2]$. We have\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})\\frac{1}{x - 1} = + ∞$,\nso $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$. Therefore, $f(x)$ is *not* continuous on the interval $[-1,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[-1,0]$. The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ is at $x=1$, which is not in the interval $[-1,0]$. Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-1,0]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, of the two intervals, the extreme value theorem is only applicable on $[-1,0]$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186175015 17592186175016 17592186175017 17592186175018 17592186175019] "immutable_fields" [[17592186175014 :answer-field/correct] [17592186175014 :answer-field/id] [17592186175014 :answer-field/key] [17592186175014 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
